{
  "requirement": "The authoring harness MUST use Rig and support Bedrock and Ollama. Users MUST be\nable to select a backend and model through configuration without changing the\nassessment tools, prompt, or evidence format.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: `requirements.authoring.provider` is satisfied.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- `RigProvider::connect` (`src/authoring/providers.rs:79`) matches on `BackendConfig`. For Bedrock it builds an `aws_sdk_bedrockruntime` client and wraps it with `rig_bedrock::client::BedrockRuntime`. For Ollama it uses `rig_core::providers::ollama::OllamaConfig`. Both are turned into a type-erased `DynModel<Completion>`, so Rig is used for both required backends. The model name comes from the configuration (`config.model`).\n- `RigProvider::complete` (`providers.rs:141`) is the single completion path. It only adds backend options and, for Bedrock, a normalization step. It does not touch the prompt, tools or evidence.\n- `normalize_bedrock_client_calls` (`providers.rs:162`) recovers client tool calls from Bedrock responses that Rig otherwise treats as opaque hosted-tool blocks. It exists so the same assessment loop works on Bedrock.\n- `configured_record` (`src/authoring/mod.rs:314`) maps the resolved configuration to a backend and model, records both, and connects the provider. It then calls the backend-agnostic `assess` loop through the `CompletionProvider` trait, which is itself marked `{impl}` (`providers.rs:13`). The prompt (`initial_prompt`), tool definitions (`tool_definitions`) and evidence JSON are built in `mod.rs` independently of the backend. That makes backend and model purely a configuration choice.\n- The code I read is covered by `{impl}` markers for this or a sibling authoring requirement:\n  - `ProviderBackoff` is marked `provider_backoff`.\n  - `bedrock_sdk_config` is marked `rig_configuration_mapping`, `configuration_precedence` and `credentials`.\n  - `apply_bedrock_options` and `option_type` are marked `configuration_storage` and `rig_configuration_mapping`.\n\n  I did not find unmarked behavior outside the requirement.\n\n**Tests**\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs` runs the real CLI against a local mock server with an Ollama configuration (the default `Fixture`). The Ollama test (`ollama_cli_delivers_sources_…`) asserts that the prompt, the tool set (`habeas`, `list_files`, `outline`, `references`, `ripgrep`, `submit_judgment`) and the evidence format are as expected.\n- `bedrock_cli_uses_selected_profile_region_and_converse_tools` runs the same CLI with a Bedrock configuration. It checks the profile, region and model path, the options mapping, the same prompt text and the same six tools, and that the evidence has the same shape. It runs both untyped and typed `tool_use` responses.\n- `authoring_uses_nearest_workspace_configuration_without_a_user_file` shows that the configured model name (`workspace-model`) reaches the request and the usage log.\n- The unit tests `typed_client_calls_preserve_order_and_native_history` and `malformed_typed_client_calls_are_reported` cover the Bedrock normalization, including ordering, idempotence, hosted or unknown tool types, and malformed calls.\n- `tests/live_bedrock.rs` is an optional live end-to-end Bedrock test. It skips when `CI` is set.\n\nThe design space is covered: both backends, model selection, and invariance of tools, prompt and evidence. No acknowledged gaps (FIXME) are present for this requirement.",
